Q1.  
The loop will run forever because `x` will eventually reach a value where adding `1.0` no longer changes it (due to floating-point precision limits). At that point, `x` will stay at a large finite number (around `1.8e308`), which is still less than `math.inf`. So the program will never print anything — it will hang in an infinite loop.

---

Q2.  
This is different. Multiplying by `1.001` will eventually cause `x` to overflow to `inf` (since the exponent grows without bound). Once `x` becomes `inf`, the condition `x < math.inf` is false, so the loop exits and prints `inf`. So the output will be `inf`.

---

Q3.  
Yes, there are inputs that will lead to "Done".  
- If `inc` is `0.0`, then `x` never changes, so `x` stays `0.0` forever, which is less than `inf` — infinite loop. Wait, that's not "Done".  
- If `inc` is `inf`, then `x` becomes `inf` immediately, so the loop exits and prints "Done".  
- If `inc` is `nan`, then `x` becomes `nan` immediately, and `nan < inf` is `False`, so the loop exits and prints "Done".  
- If `inc` is a very large finite number like `1e308`, then `x` will overflow to `inf` after a few additions, so "Done" will be printed.  

So examples: `inf`, `nan`, `1e308`. [end of text]


